savestr: each save box entry's name its own buffer (SparkFSApp's c/file).

saveblock[] gives each entry's name as a string literal -- ten of them
"            " (twelve spaces), the archive types' entries -- and
setuparctypes() strcpy()s each archive type's name into its entry's.
Norcroft's code keeps each of those literals apart, and RISC OS lets an
application write to them, so that works there; clang merges identical literals into one (and puts
it in read-only data), so every entry's name was the same twelve bytes,
the last archive type's: the New archive save box offered "Zip" five
times where RISC OS 5.30 offers Spark file, Spark dir, PK arc, Tar, Zip.
names is now a 13-byte array in each entry, initialised from the same
literal: what Norcroft's layout gave each one.

diff -ru a/SparkFSApp/file.c b/SparkFSApp/file.c
--- a/SparkFSApp/file.c	2026-10-02 09:26:54.815749588 +0100
+++ b/SparkFSApp/file.c	2026-10-02 09:26:54.819417397 +0100
@@ -118,7 +118,7 @@
 
 typedef struct savestr
 {
- char * names;
+ char   names[13];   /* ROSGD: each entry its own (the patch says why) */
  int    type;
  extfn  fn;
 } savestr;
